# Appendix J — The Six Teaching Shapes

Made by Alfred Borden.

There is a difference between knowing what a verb *does* and knowing how to
*teach* it. You can read a thousand folds and still not know how to hand `fold`
to someone else. This appendix is the seed bank for that second skill: six
*shapes* — six ways of turning one verb into a runnable lesson. Every shape
below is anchored on the same verb, `fold`, so you can watch the shape change
while the subject holds still. And every block on this page runs at the prompt.
Type it, watch the answer come to be, then read why it came.

The six shapes are:

| # | Shape | The question it answers |
|---|-------|-------------------------|
| 0 | verb-identity | *What is this verb, across the whole grid?* |
| 1 | run-and-explain | *What happens when I run it — step by step?* |
| 2 | decompose-and-label | *What are its parts, and what is each called?* |
| 3 | build-up ladder | *How do I climb to it, one runnable rung at a time?* |
| 4 | reversals | *Can I go backward — words to code, broken to fixed?* |
| 5 | comprehension-vocabulary | *What are the words for the pieces, so I can say the shape out loud?* |

## Shape 0 — verb-identity: the whole grid in one cell

The identity of a verb is not one line. It is its full grid — many small
programs, in engineering, in math, in computer science. Show a learner a single
cell and the lesson grows outward into *what is `fold`, really?* Here is one
cell: fold `+` over a list with seed `0`.

```motoi
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))
```

```
10
```

That single answer, `10`, is a doorway. The seed `0` went in, then `1`, then
`2`, then `3`, then `4`, each one combined with the running total by `+`. A kid
sees a machine that eats a list and hands back one thing. A working programmer
sees *reduce a column of numbers to a total*. A mathematician sees the
catamorphism — the one fold that every other fold is a special case of. Same
cell, three heights. That is what verb-identity means: one runnable seed, grown
to fit whoever is asking.

## Shape 1 — run-and-explain: watch the answer come to be

Predict first. *Then* run. *Then* trace the run, one step at a time. The whole
lesson is the watching. Fold `cons` over a list, starting from the empty list:

```motoi
(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

```
(5 4 3 2 1)
```

It reversed! Why? Because Motoi's `fold` is element-first: it hands each element
to the combiner *before* the running answer. So `(cons 1 '())` makes `(1)`, then
`(cons 2 '(1))` makes `(2 1)`, then `(cons 3 '(2 1))` makes `(3 2 1)`, and on to
`(5 4 3 2 1)`. Each number jumps to the front of the line as it arrives. Predict
the reversal, run it, then trace it — and now you *own* the reason instead of
memorizing the result.

## Shape 2 — decompose-and-label: name the parts, then swap one

A fold has three slots: a *combiner* (what to combine with), a *seed* (what to
start from), and a *list* (what to fold). Name them, and you can swap one on
purpose. Here the parts are `+`, `0`, and `xs`, wrapped in a named verb:

```motoi
(define (sum xs) (fold + 0 xs))
(sum (list 1 2 3 4))
```

```
10
```

The *signature* is `(list-of number) -> number`. The *inventory* is: combiner
`+`, seed `0`, list `xs`. The *template* is `(fold <combiner> <seed> <list>)` —
and once you can see the template, you can build a different organ from the same
skeleton. Keep the template, swap the combiner from `+` to `cons` and the seed
from `0` to `'()`, and `sum` becomes `reverse`. Labels are what make a
deliberate swap possible instead of a lucky guess.

## Shape 3 — build-up ladder: one runnable rung at a time

You do not have to hold the whole program in your head. Climb to it. Each rung
runs on its own, and each wraps the last in exactly one new idea:
generate → filter → reduce → abstract.

```motoi
(iota 6)
```

```
(0 1 2 3 4 5)
```

```motoi
(filter odd? (iota 6))
```

```
(1 3 5)
```

```motoi
(fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota 6)))
```

```
9
```

```motoi
(define (sum-odd n) (fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota n))))
(sum-odd 10)
```

```
25
```

First you *generate* a list. Then you *filter* it down to the odds. Then you
*reduce* the survivors to one number. Then you *abstract* the whole pipeline
into a verb that takes `n`. Build it like the console builds a game — one brick,
run, next brick. Never carry the whole tower at once.

## Shape 4 — reversals: words to code, broken to fixed

Two directions most lessons skip. First, from *words* to *code*: read a
sentence, write the program that means it. \"Sum the squares of 1 through 5\":

```motoi
(fold + 0 (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (iota 5 1)))
```

```
55
```

Second, from *broken* to *fixed* — and some seeds **must fail**, because a
failure that happens for the right reason is itself a lesson. Watch this one
break:

```motoi
(fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3))
```

```
unbound symbol: fold-left
```

That is not a typo in the book. `fold-left` is the R6RS/Racket name, and Motoi
leaves it deliberately unbound — so the interpreter itself refuses it and hands
you a free correctness gate. The fix is the SRFI-1 name Motoi *does* carry:

```motoi
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))
```

```
6
```

Here is a second break, a different kind. `filter` wants a *predicate* in its
first slot — a question that answers yes or no — not a plain value:

```motoi
(filter 42 (iota 6))
```

```
not a function: 42
```

Read the error; it names the mistake exactly. `42` is not a function, so
`filter` cannot ask it anything. Give it a real predicate and it works:

```motoi
(filter even? (iota 6))
```

```
(0 2 4)
```

Going backward — code to description, and broken to fixed — is where a learner
stops copying and starts understanding. The error class is the lesson, not the
exit code.

## Shape 5 — comprehension-vocabulary: the words for the pieces

The last shape gives the other five their language. When you can *name* the
pieces, the shape becomes something you can say out loud, ask about, and teach.
Here is a fold with a filter inside it:

```motoi
(fold + 0 (filter odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
```

```
9
```

Point and name. The `+` is the **combiner**. The `0` is the **seed**. The
`odd?` is the **predicate**. The whole fold is **pure** — no effect, the same
answer every single time you run it. And `fold` recurs in **tail position**,
which is why it can walk a long list without growing the stack (its cousin
`fold-right` does not). Five words — combiner, seed, predicate, pure,
tail-position — and suddenly every one of the other five shapes is teachable,
because now you have the vocabulary to point at what you mean.

---

That is the seed bank. Six shapes, one verb, every block runnable. Hand any of
them to someone at the console and it grows into a whole conversation — which is
exactly what these anchors are for."
)
